Max. kinetic energy of photoelectrons $=4 \mathrm{eV}$
If stopping potential is $V_0$, then
$\begin{aligned}
& & e V_0 & =4 \mathrm{eV} \\
& & V_0 & =4 \mathrm{volt}
\end{aligned}$

$E=E_0 \cos (k x-\omega t)\therefore \quad \omega=2 \pi \times 6 \times 10^{14}\begin{array}{ll}
\therefore & f=\frac{\omega}{2 \pi}=\frac{2 \pi \times 6 \times 10^{14}}{2 \pi}=6 \times 10^{14} \mathrm{~Hz} \\
& \phi=\text { Energy of photon }=\frac{1240}{\lambda(\text { in } \mathrm{nm})} \mathrm{eV} \\
\therefore & \lambda=\frac{c}{f}=\frac{3 \times 10^8}{6 \times 10^{14}}=0.5 \times 10^{-6} \mathrm{~m}=500 \mathrm{~nm} \\
\therefore & \phi=\frac{1240}{500} \mathrm{eV}=2.48 \mathrm{eV}
\end{array} \\ Now, by Einstein photoelectric equation,
$\begin{array}{rlrl} 
& & \phi & =\phi_0+\mathrm{KE}=\phi_0+e V_s \quad\left(\because \mathrm{KE}=e V_s\right) \\
\Rightarrow & 2.48 & =1.5+e V_s \\
& & e V_s & =2.48-1.5=0.98 \mathrm{eV} \\
& V_s & =0.98 \mathrm{~V}
\end{array}
